title: OVAL and SCAP, hands-on
guide: howto/oval-and-scap.html
promise: Read OVAL, the XML format Linux vendors publish security advisories in: its four building blocks, why backported patches break version checks, and a real scan with the OpenSCAP scanner.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for intermediate readers comfortable at a Linux command line who can run a Docker container and know what a vulnerability scanner reports and what a CVE, a publicly numbered vulnerability, is. Allow about two hours.

You need Docker.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read everything before the walk-through in order. Paste BASH blocks with docker commands into your own terminal, the rest into the Fedora container. XML blocks are OVAL to read. Comments say what to look for. Run step 9's clean-up last.

== Set up: start a Fedora container
section: 1. Get a scanner
The OpenSCAP scanner, oscap, is Linux-only, so it runs in a Fedora container. In your own terminal, this makes ~/oval-lab and starts the container with that folder as /work, so reports survive when it exits. Expect a bash prompt.
```bash
mkdir -p ~/oval-lab && cd ~/oval-lab

docker run --rm -it -v "$PWD:/work" -w /work fedora:latest bash
```

== Set up: install the scanner and its content
section: 1. Get a scanner
At that prompt, this installs oscap and the compliance content under /usr/share/xml/scap/. Then oscap --version prints the schema versions it supports. Read them: content written against a newer schema than your scanner understands is a real and confusing failure mode.
```bash
dnf install -y openscap-scanner scap-security-guide bzip2 libxml2 && oscap --version
```

== What OVAL is, and why you read it
section: What OVAL is, and why you will read it rather than write it
OVAL, the Open Vulnerability and Assessment Language, is XML stating what must be true of a system for a claim to hold. Every major Linux distribution publishes advisories in it. You read it, usually when your scanner and vendor disagree.

== Where OVAL sits inside SCAP
section: Where OVAL sits inside SCAP
SCAP, the Security Content Automation Protocol, is a family of compliance standards.
- XCCDF (Extensible Configuration Checklist Description Format) is the checklist.
- Each rule points at the OVAL definition deciding pass or fail.
- A data stream bundles them in one file.

== The one idea: four elements joined by id
section: The four building blocks
An object says what to look at and a state says what would be true. A test, like this illustrative one, binds one object to one state by their ids. A definition's criteria combine tests with AND, OR and NOT.
```xml
<!-- TEST: check="at least one" means the test passes if any installed
     package matching the object satisfies the state. -->
<rpminfo_test id="oval:com.redhat.rhsa:tst:20231234002" check="at least one"
              comment="openssl is earlier than 1:3.0.7-6.el9_1" version="1">
  <object object_ref="oval:com.redhat.rhsa:obj:20231234002"/>
  <state  state_ref="oval:com.redhat.rhsa:ste:20231234002"/>
</rpminfo_test>
```

== Backports: the fix is in the release field
section: One definition, shaped like a real vendor advisory
Vendors routinely backport, patching an older version instead of shipping the original project's newer one. This state's evr (epoch, version, release) puts the fix in the release field, -6.el9_1; 3.0.7 never moved. Scanners matching against that newer version are wrong.
```xml
<rpminfo_state id="oval:com.redhat.rhsa:ste:20231234002" version="1">
  <evr datatype="evr_string" operation="less than">1:3.0.7-6.el9_1</evr>
</rpminfo_state>
```

== Step 2: download Red Hat's OVAL feed
section: 2. Fetch a real vendor OVAL feed
A vendor's OVAL feed is one file holding its definitions for one release. This downloads Red Hat's feed for Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) 9, which steps 3, 4 and 8 read, and counts its definitions.
```bash
# Red Hat publishes per-release OVAL streams; Debian, Ubuntu, and SUSE
# publish equivalents. The download is about 1.5 MB, and bunzip2 unpacks
# it to more than 35 MB. Both grow as Red Hat publishes advisories.
curl -sSLO https://access.redhat.com/security/data/oval/v2/RHEL9/rhel-9.oval.xml.bz2
bunzip2 -f rhel-9.oval.xml.bz2

# How many definitions did we just download?
grep -c '<definition ' rhel-9.oval.xml
```

== Step 3: follow a definition with xmllint
section: 3. Read one definition end to end
The xmllint --xpath command prints what an XPath expression, a path through a document's elements, selects. This prints the ids of definitions citing CVE-2023-0286. Step 3's second command looks up each id you find: definition, test, object, state.
```bash
# Find a definition that references a CVE you care about.
xmllint --xpath \
  "//*[local-name()='definition'][.//*[@ref_id='CVE-2023-0286']]/@id" \
  rhel-9.oval.xml

# Note local-name(): OVAL is heavily namespaced, and a plain //definition
# XPath silently returns nothing. Empty output from a namespaced document
# almost always means the namespace, not an absent element.
```

== Step 4: every definition comes back true
section: 4. Run an OVAL-only evaluation
On Fedora every definition is true. Each opens with an OR whose first test, "Red Hat Enterprise Linux must be installed", passes when the package owning /etc/redhat-release is not redhat-release. Read a vendor feed's results only on its platform.
```bash
oscap oval eval --results oval-results.xml --report oval-report.html \
  rhel-9.oval.xml > oval-eval.txt

# Count the definitions that came back true, and compare with the number
# of definitions step 2 counted in the feed.
grep -c ': true' oval-eval.txt

# Which package owns the file that Red Hat's platform check reads?
rpm -qf /etc/redhat-release
```

== Step 5: a compliance scan, and its exit codes
section: 5. Run a real compliance scan through XCCDF
Step 5's first block runs oscap info, which lists a data stream's profiles, named baselines; never guess a profile id. This scan writes ARF (Asset Reporting Format) results. Exit code 2 means it found problems; 1 means it never happened.
```bash
oscap xccdf eval \
  --profile xccdf_org.ssgproject.content_profile_standard \
  --results-arf arf.xml \
  --report report.html \
  /usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content/ssg-fedora-ds.xml

echo $?   # 0 = all rules passed, 2 = at least one failed, 1 = the scan errored
```

== Step 6: pull the failing rules from the ARF
section: 6. Read the results
This lists up to 20 failing rule ids. Each maps back to an XCCDF rule with prose rationale, which points at the OVAL definition that produced the verdict. When someone disputes a finding, that chain is the evidence.
```bash
# Open report.html in a browser for the human view. For the machine view,
# pull the rule results straight out of the ARF:
xmllint --xpath \
  "//*[local-name()='rule-result'][*[local-name()='result']='fail']/@idref" \
  arf.xml | tr ' ' '\n' | head -20
```

== Step 8: export an old image on your computer
section: 8. Scan a container image instead of a host
In a second terminal on your own computer, this downloads the RHEL 9 Universal Base Image (UBI) 9.0.0, built in 2022 and missing years of fixes, writes its filesystem to ubi9-old.tar, about 240 MB, and removes the container it made.
```bash
cd ~/oval-lab
docker create --name ubi9-old registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi:9.0.0
docker export ubi9-old -o ubi9-old.tar
docker rm ubi9-old
```

== Step 8: scan the image's files
section: 8. Scan a container image instead of a host
In the container, oscap-chroot, from openscap-utils, runs step 4's evaluation on the files under /ubi9-old, and grep counts advisories that apply. The scan itself exits 0 either way. As a control, ubi:latest, exported and scanned the same way, counts 0.
```bash
dnf install -y openscap-utils
mkdir -p /ubi9-old && tar -xf ubi9-old.tar -C /ubi9-old
oscap-chroot /ubi9-old oval eval --report image-report.html rhel-9.oval.xml > image-scan.txt
grep -c ': true' image-scan.txt
```

== Exercise: the scanner or the vendor?
section: Hands-on exercises
Your RHEL 9 host has openssl-3.0.7-6.el9_1. A scanner flags an openssl CVE, "fixed in 3.0.8"; the vendor's OVAL definition, with fixed evr 1:3.0.7-6.el9_1, is false. Who is right, and why? The guide hides answers under "Show the answer".

== Answer: the vendor, because of the backport
section: Hands-on exercises
The vendor is right: Red Hat backported the fix into 3.0.7's release field, so you are not vulnerable. The scanner compared 3.0.7 with 3.0.8. Running rpm -q --changelog openssl | grep CVE- lists the CVEs the package claims to fix.

== Exercise: could the XCCDF alone decide?
section: Hands-on exercises
Take one failing rule id from step 6 and find the OVAL definition behind it. Then answer: could you have determined pass or fail by reading the XCCDF alone?

== Answer: no, OVAL says how to decide
section: Hands-on exercises
No: XCCDF says what should be true and why, for a human; OVAL says how to determine it, for a machine. A rule with no check reference reports notchecked and never fails a scan. Replace <rule-id> below with yours.
```bash
# The XCCDF rule carries a <check-content-ref> naming the OVAL definition id.
xmllint --xpath "//*[local-name()='Rule'][@id='<rule-id>']
  //*[local-name()='check-content-ref']/@name" \
  /usr/share/xml/scap/ssg/content/ssg-fedora-ds.xml
```

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- Step 7 generates a remediation script. Review it like a pull request, never pipe it into a shell: its baseline never saw your workload.
- Switch rules off with a tailoring file, a separate XCCDF file, not by editing data streams.
